# netsurf: import() loads its module graph between frames (no more freeze)
#
# Roadmap WS-C1. QuickJS loads an import()'s graph synchronously inside
# the job the call queues, and the bridge's loader fetched (blocking curl)
# and compiled all of it there: claude.ai's 91 dynamic modules froze the
# Vita for ~54 s (hardware run 22) -- no frames, no input.
#
# - patches/quickjs/03 adds JS_SetModuleDeferFunc: the host may keep an
#   import() job and re-queue it (JS_EnqueueJob) when its graph is loaded.
# - fetch.h/fetch.c/curl.c: fetch_get_set_{start,step,body,free}, the
#   non-blocking twin of fetch_get_blocking (same transfers, 8 at a time,
#   each step returns at once).
# - macsurf_qjs.c: qjs_module_defer keeps the job; a pipeline per import
#   root (qjs_modload) then, a few steps per event-loop pass:
#   FETCH the graph level by level with fetch_get_set (card hits
#   registered at once; a module another pipeline is fetching is carried to
#   the next level instead of fetched twice), COMPILE the fetched modules
#   one after another in 12 ms slices (each lands in the realm's loaded
#   list, so QuickJS does not ask the loader again; not while another
#   pipeline on the registry still fetches, as compiling frees the text it
#   may not have scanned yet), then re-queue the jobs. A failed root is
#   handed back to QuickJS, which rejects as before. Pipelines die with
#   their realm (navigation, teardown). The blocking crawl and the loader
#   now share qjs_crawl_level_{want,take,next} and qjs_module_compile_entry
#   with it.
# - import() from a classic (inline) script resolves against the document
#   URL, as in browsers (qjs_module_base): './x.js' could not load at all.
# Sim, claude.ai/code: its import() graphs load asynchronously, the longest
# compile slice 151 ms, the redirect to /login at 6.4 s (as before).
# Test: JS-module-async.html 4/4 (timers run while the graph loads).
